We analyze the mathematical modeling of electrostatic MEMS devices through general differential equations, given by:

\begin{equation}
\begin{cases}
\varphi\big(r,u'(r)\big) = \lambda\int_0^r\frac{f(s)}{g(u(s))}\,\mathrm{d}s & r\in(0,1) \\
0<u(r)<1 & r\in(0,1) \\
u(1) = 0 & 
\end{cases}
\end{equation}

where $\varphi$, $g$, $f$ are certain functions on $[0,1]$, and $\lambda>0$ is a parameter. Results on the existence, regularity and characteristics of touchdown solutions for this equation have been found, and upper and lower bounds on the corresponding pull-in voltage are provided. Specifically, for $\varphi(r,v) = r^\alpha |v|^\beta v$, an asymptotic description of the touchdown solution close to the origin has been established.

We derive the conditions that ensure a well-posed Cauchy problem is established in (Muckenhoupt) weighted L_p-spaces for the equation 
$\frac{\partial u}{\partial t}(t,x)=\psi(t,i\nabla)u(t,x)+f(t,x)$, with a time-measurable pseudo-differential operator given by $\psi(t,i\nabla)u(t,x):=\mathcal{F}^{-1}\left[\psi(t,\cdot)\mathcal{F}[u](t,\cdot)\right](x)$ and the initial condition u(0,x)=0. This is done by identifying the properties of the symbol ψ(t,ξ) that guarantee well-posedness, specifically its smoothness with respect to ξ, which varies based on the characteristics of the weights involved. Consequently, we obtain bounds for any solution u to the equation, including \begin{equation}
  \label{ab est*} \int_0^T \int_{\mathbb{R}^d} |(-\Delta)^{\gamma/2} u(t,x) |^p
(t^2 + |x|^2)^{\alpha/2} dxdt \leq N\int_0^T \int_{\mathbb{R}^d} |f(t,x)|^p
(t^2 + |x|^2)^{\alpha/2} dxdt \end{equation} and \begin{equation}
  \label{ab est* 2} \int_0^T \left(\int_{\mathbb{R}^d} |(-\Delta)^{\gamma/2}
u(t,x) |^p |x|^{\alpha_2} dx \right)^{q/p} t^{\alpha_1}dt \leq N\int_0^T
\left(\int_{\mathbb{R}^d} |f(t,x) |^p |x|^{\alpha_2} dx \right)^{q/p}
t^{\alpha_1}dt. \end{equation} The parameters are restricted such that $p,q\in(1,\infty)$, $-d-1<\alpha < (d+1)(p-1)$, $-1 < \alpha_1 < q-1$, $-d <\alpha_2< d(p-1)$, and the operator's order $\gamma$ satisfies certain conditions.
